module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.recursion_over_value_depth_uncounted_by_the_call_limit

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data recursion_over_value_depth_uncounted_by_the_call_limit: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "recursion_over_value_depth_uncounted_by_the_call_limit" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: host-native recursion whose depth is the depth of a VALUE rather than the depth of an interpreted call chain. The seed interpreter bounds calls (`v1_interpreter` `CALL_DEPTH_LIMIT`, refused typed and located) and runs every call and closure frame under `stacker::maybe_grow`, but a value is as deep as the data it carries -- a right-nested parse of a large module is one Variant per token -- and the derived `Drop`, `PartialEq`, `Display` and `Debug` of `Value`, plus every hand-written walker over it, recursed once per level on the host stack with no checkpoint. No call limit counts those levels, so none could refuse them.",

    "HARM: process death with no location (rc=134, `thread main has overflowed its stack`), below DESIGN section 5's floor: not a refusal, not a diagnostic, and fatal to every later claim in the same batch. The drop case is the sharpest: the claim had already printed PASS and the process died afterwards, while releasing the value.",

    "THE PREMISE IT WAS REPORTED UNDER WAS FALSE, MEASURED RATHER THAN ARGUED (2026-10-01, seed at main b793732902, BuildBuddy): the report was that 100,000 typed frames overflow the native stack. Non-tail interpreted recursion at 99,990 PASSED and at 100,010 refused `call depth exceeded 100000 at fn 'down'`, typed and located -- the call limit was already true. In the same dispatch, dropping a 262,144-deep variant chain aborted rc=134 after its claim passed, and `==` on two such chains aborted rc=134. The trigger (a census lens over v2.std.compilers.target_model) builds exactly such a value.",

    "CHAIN (DESIGN section 6b): the earliest unjustified link is each recursion over value structure, not the call path and not the thread's stack size. Value depth is bounded by the heap, so no derived thread stack suffices, and a drop cannot refuse. REPAIR: `Drop for Value` is iterative -- uniquely owned children are detached onto a heap worklist, shared ones left to their last owner. Every other walker runs under the single guard `v1_interpreter` `value_depth_guarded` (`stacker::maybe_grow`, the mechanism calls already use): PartialEq, Display, a hand-written Debug matching the derived output, value_hash, value_fast_eq, the portable conversions, account_value, cross_representation_numeric_straddle, json_to_value, match_pattern, push_shell_argv_tokens, the fixture codec and cli_run's dotted-string, node-value and wire walkers. The dead recursive encoder `value_to_json` (no production caller) is deleted.",

    "THE JSON BOUNDARY IS A REFUSAL, NOT A GUARD, because the recursion there is upstream: serde_json::Value's own Drop and Serializer recurse per level beyond any guard of ours. Both encoders (`recorded_fixture` `value_to_fixture_json`, `cli_run` `value_to_wire_json`) claim each JSON container through `v1_interpreter` `claim_json_nesting` before building it and refuse, naming the value path, past `serde_json_read_depth_limit` -- PROBED once as the deepest nesting serde_json::from_str admits (127 on serde_json 1.0.149, not the 128 a transcribed literal would have said). For the fixture encoder the bound is a projection of its reader (serde_json::from_slice). For the wire encoder it is a stated POLICY bound: its in-process readers decode with serde_json::from_value into non-recursive typed structs (no depth limit of their own), and its REST request bodies go to external services whose nesting limit is undocumented (GitHub, Google Cloud APIs searched; none found). The justification is that the encoder itself dies above it.",

    "EVIDENCE, SPLIT BY COST. RUNTIME (v1-compiler lib, test modules `v1_interpreter` `value_depth_walker_tests` and `cli_run` `collect_node_values_depth_tests`, 2 MiB test-thread stack): a 262,144-deep chain and list are driven through every walker above; the JSON encoders refuse it located while the process lives; a value at exactly the probed limit encodes and its bytes read back, and one level deeper refuses. These are named in the population of gunbc.rung_drop rust_unit_tests_off_the_merge_path by the amendment gunbc.rung_drop_amendment value_depth_runtime_tests_named_in_the_population, because they run on no CI path and cannot move to the floor: building a value that deep costs one interpreted step per level, past the eval-step budget, and a shallower value does not overflow and so discriminates nothing. STRUCTURAL: the census of unguarded recursive Value walkers (`unguarded_recursive_value_walkers`, in v1-compiler's `src/v1/stage0/build.rs` -- not under src/, where every file is a rostered stage0 surface and a census would be new seed growth) PARSES every source file with syn and REFUSES THE BUILD when a function taking the interpreter's `Value` calls itself OUTSIDE a closure handed to `value_depth_guarded` -- the guard must lexically wrap the recursive call, so a comment, a string or an unrelated mention cannot satisfy it (review 73634 found the first, text-grep version satisfiable that way, and this replaced it). A file that does not parse refuses. Every lane that compiles the crate -- the required lint step among them -- enforces it on every merge. Measured red on BuildBuddy: stripping the guard from value_fast_eq fails `cargo check` naming exactly that walker, and so does stripping it while a comment and a string literal in the body name `value_depth_guarded`. The parse found two walkers the grep had missed because their Value sits inside a generic (bind_argv_expr, hoist_call_arg_string_literal_edges), and the grep found match_pattern and push_shell_argv_tokens that the hand census had missed; all are guarded. The parse runs on a thread whose stack is sized from each file's measured bracket nesting (the generated emitter mirror nests ~2,000 deep and overflowed the build script's default stack)",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: outside the ladder (silent process death). RUNG NOW: 2, mechanically preventable, on the merge path for the structural class -- a new directly self-recursive walker over Value without the guard does not build. Two caveats, stated: the census sees DIRECT self-recursion only -- free functions and methods calling themselves (`name(..)`, `Self::name(..)`, `self.name(..)`) over a parameter whose type names `Value` outside serde_json; a mutually recursive pair, recursion through an operator (`==` reaching PartialEq), or a Value reached through a type alias is not detected (PartialEq, Display and Debug are guarded by hand and Drop is iterative), and the deep-value runtime evidence is off the merge path under the drop named above.",

    "ATTAINABLE CEILING: 3 for the interpreter (a walker over Value that cannot be written without the guard needs a single traversal authority every walker is an instance of). NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, named as the capability: one generic Value traversal (an iterative fold over children) through which every walker is expressed, so a recursive walker has no other way to be written; and the rust_unit_tests_off_the_merge_path drop's own trigger, which puts this evidence on the merge path."
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
